tel 1.0

base escapade
import soundness

info   styled terminal text as a value: the `e"…"` interpolator, `Teletype`, and ANSI rendering by capability
detail
    _Escapade_ makes styled terminal output a **value** rather than a stream of escape codes.
    A `Teletype` is text which carries a style — colour, weight, underline, hyperlink — for
    every character, built with the `e"…"` interpolator and rendered only at the moment of
    output, to whatever the receiving terminal can show: 24-bit colour on a capable terminal,
    the nearest of 256 colours on an older one, and plain text when the output is not a
    terminal at all, so a log file never fills with escape sequences.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
    import termcapDefinitions.xtermTrueColorTermcap

    val message: Teletype = e"$Bold(Warning): ${Fg(WebColors.Red)}(disk full)"
    Out.println(message)
    message.render(termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap)   // t"Warning: disk full"
    ```

    Inside `e"…"`, a substituted `Bold`, `Italic`, `Underline`, `Strike`, `Reverse`, `Conceal`,
    `Faint`, `DoubleUnderline`, `BlinkSlow`, `BlinkFast` or `Overline` marker, an `Fg` or `Bg`
    colour, any `Chromatic` colour, an `Escape` pair or a `Hyperlink`, followed by a bracketed
    span — `(…)`, `[…]`, `{…}`, `<…>` or `«…»` — styles that span, and spans nest: each
    substitution is a *transformation* of the prevailing `TextStyle` (a `Stylize` instance),
    pushed on a stack and popped at the closing bracket, so an inner colour gives way to the
    outer one again where it ends, which raw ANSI cannot express. Any other value is
    substituted as text, through its `Teletypeable` or `Showable` instance, keeping its own
    styling if it is already a `Teletype`. An unclosed bracket is a compile error.

    A `Teletype` is `Textual`, so everything gossamer does with `Text` — `keep`, `skip`,
    `pad`, `fit`, `cut`, `join`, `trim`, `upper`, `words`, `lines`, `reverse`, … — works on
    it with the styling preserved, and it is `Printable`, so `Out.println` renders it
    through the `Termcap` of the `Stdio` in scope.

    The library is split into four source sets:

    - `escapade.csi` — the `csi` object, which spells the cursor, erase and SGR control
      sequences as `Text`, and `unstyled`, which strips every control sequence from text
      that already contains them. It depends only on gossamer.
    - `escapade.core` — `Teletype`, the `e"…"` interpolator and its `Ansi` machinery, the
      style markers, `Fg`, `Bg`, `TextStyle`, `Stylize`, `Teletypeable`, `Colorable`,
      `Imprintable`, `Ribbon`, `Hyperlink`, `Escape`, the raw `escapes` table and the
      `displayableTypes` package.
    - `escapade.io` — the `writables` package: streams of `Teletype` records written to
      `Out` and `Err`.
    - `escapade.graphics` — the `teletypeables` package: any `Graphical` image rendered as
      half-block characters.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `displayableTypes`,
    `writables` and `teletypeables` choice packages merge with the umbrella's own, so a user
    writes `import soundness.*` and imports the givens of those packages by name, since
    `import soundness.*` does not import givens. The five newer markers (`Faint`,
    `DoubleUnderline`, `BlinkSlow`, `BlinkFast`, `Overline`) and `StyleWord` are not among
    the umbrella's exports, and are reached through the `escapade` package itself.

refer escapade.Teletype
refer escapade.e
refer escapade.Stylize
refer escapade.Teletypeable
refer escapade.Fg
refer escapade.Bg

entry .Teletype   text with a style for every character, rendered to a terminal's capabilities at output
  detail
      A `Teletype` is the value the `e"…"` interpolator makes: its `#plain` text, and for
      every character a 64-bit `StyleWord` packing foreground, background and eleven
      attribute flags, held either densely (one word per character) or as runs of equal
      style (`#boundaries` marks where each run starts). Hyperlinks (OSC 8) and raw escape
      insertions are kept beside the text by position, so the plain text is exactly what the
      terminal will show and its length is the width it occupies.

      Nothing is rendered until `#render` is called with a `Termcap`: a terminal without ANSI
      support gets the plain text, one with 256 colours gets the nearest colour of the cube,
      and a true-colour terminal gets 24-bit colour. The `Printable` given does this for
      `Out.println`, through the `Termcap` the `Stdio` in scope reports.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Plain text
        code
            import soundness.*
            import termcapDefinitions.xtermTrueColorTermcap

            val line = e"disk full"
            line.render(xtermTrueColorTermcap)   // t"disk full"
      frame
        label A span in bold
        code
            import soundness.*
            import termcapDefinitions.xtermTrueColorTermcap

            val line = e"$Bold(Warning): disk full"
            line.render(xtermTrueColorTermcap)   // t"\e[1mWarning\e[22m: disk full"
      frame
        label A coloured span, nested inside it
        code
            import soundness.*
            import termcapDefinitions.xtermTrueColorTermcap

            val line = e"$Bold(Warning: ${Fg(WebColors.Red)}(disk full))"
            line.render(xtermTrueColorTermcap)   // the red ends before the bold does
      frame
        label A hyperlink around the whole line
        code
            import soundness.*
            import termcapDefinitions.xtermTrueColorTermcap

            val line = e"${Hyperlink(t"https://example.com/disk")}[$Bold(Warning: ${Fg(WebColors.Red)}(disk full))]"
            line.plain                             // t"Warning: disk full"
            line.render(xtermTrueColorTermcap)     // OSC 8 link, bold and red
            line.render(termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap)   // t"Warning: disk full"
      ##
      ```

      A `Teletype` is `Textual` (`.textual`), so the gossamer vocabulary applies with styling
      preserved: `keep`, `skip`, `pad`, `fit`, `center`, `trim`, `chomp`, `cut`, `words`,
      `lines`, `upper`, `lower`, `reverse`, `contains`, `starts`, `offsetOf`, and the rest.
      Two teletypes concatenate with `+` or `#append`, a `List[Teletype]` joins with `join`,
      and `#dropChars` and `#takeChars` cut by character count from either end. Equality is
      structural over every field; comparison (`.comparable`) is by plain text under a
      `Collation`.

      Construction: `Teletype(text)` for unstyled text, `Teletype.styled(value)(transform)`
      for a uniformly styled value, the `e"…"` interpolator for anything with markup, and
      `Teletype.Builder` to accumulate pieces. The case-class constructor takes the raw
      arrays and is for code which has already computed them, such as `teletypeables`
      givens.
  refer escapade.e
  refer escapade.TextStyle
  refer escapade.StyleWord
  refer escapade.Teletypeable
  entry .SparseThreshold   the ratio at which a dense style array is compressed to runs: `2`
      A teletype built by the interpolator or a `Builder` keeps one style word per character
      unless the text is at least twice as long as its number of style runs, in which case
      the runs are stored instead. The choice affects only memory and speed, never the
      rendering.
  entry .add   `left + right`: the two teletypes appended, where `Teletype` is not seen as `Textual`
      The `Addable` given, guarded by `NotGiven[Teletype is Textual]` so that it yields to
      gossamer's own `+` for textual values when `.textual` is in scope, and both spell
      `#append`.
  entry .concatenable   the `Concatenable` given: `concat(left, right)` is `left.append(right)`
  entry .measurable   the display width of a teletype is the width of its plain text
      Styling and hyperlinks occupy no cells, so under any `Text is Measurable` metric (such
      as `textMetrics.uniformMetric`, or the East Asian width metric) a teletype measures as
      its `#plain` does.
  entry .reversible   `teletype.reverse`, with the styling of each character kept
      Delegates to gossamer's `reversibleTextual`, placed here in the companion so that the
      single `reverse` of `rudiments` finds it without an import.
  entry .traversable   lets the predicate forms of `keep` and `skip` walk a teletype character by character
      Delegates to gossamer's `traversableTextual`: `e"hello world".keep(_.isLetter)` finds
      its boundary by traversal and rebuilds by `segment`, so styling survives.
  entry .textual   the `Textual` given: a `Teletype` is text whose characters are `Char`s
      Every gossamer extension on textual values applies to a teletype through this instance.
      Its `Show` type member is `Teletypeable`, so a value substituted into a teletype by a
      generic textual operation is shown through its `Teletypeable` rather than its
      `Showable`. `map` over the characters keeps every style word in place, and `segment`
      is `dropChars` then `takeChars`.
  entry .empty   the empty teletype: no text, the default trailing style
  entry .joinable   `parts.join`: many teletypes appended, in a balanced tree rather than a left fold
      Each `append` copies both operands, so a left fold over `n` parts would copy the first
      part `n` times and joining a long diagnostic was quadratic; the join splits the parts in
      halves and copies each one only about `log n` times. `append` is associative and drops
      empty parts, so the result is exactly the left fold's.
  entry .printable   the `Printable` given: `Out.println(teletype)` renders it through the `Stdio`'s `Termcap`
      `print(teletype, termcap)` is `teletype.render(termcap)`, so the escape sequences
      emitted depend on the terminal the standard output actually is.
  entry .cuttable   `teletype.cut(delimiter)`: split on text, each piece keeping its styles
      The `Cuttable by Text` given behind gossamer's `cut`, with an optional limit on the
      number of pieces. `e"a,b,c".cut(t",")` is three styled pieces, and `e"".cut(t",")` is a
      list of one empty teletype.
  entry .comparable   teletypes order by their plain text, under the `Collation` in scope
  entry .apply   `Teletype(text)`: the text, with no styling
  entry .styled   `Teletype.styled(value)(transform)`: a `Showable` value in one uniform style
      Shows the value, then applies `transform` to the default `TextStyle` and gives every
      character the resulting style, as `Teletype.styled(42.0)(_.copy(fg = Chroma(0xffd600)))`
      does for the `Double` rendering.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import termcapDefinitions.xtermTrueColorTermcap

      Teletype.styled(t"all bold")(_.copy(bold = true)).render(xtermTrueColorTermcap)
      ```
  entry .compressIfBeneficial   converts a dense style array to runs when the text is long enough to justify it
      Returns the `(styles, boundaries)` pair for the text: the arrays unchanged with empty
      boundaries if the runs would not save space (by `.SparseThreshold`), or the run styles
      with their start positions otherwise. For code which builds a teletype from its own
      style array and wants the representation the interpolator would have chosen.
  entry .Builder   accumulates teletypes, texts and characters into one `Teletype`
    detail
        A `gossamer.Builder[Teletype]`, which is what `Teletype.textual.builder` returns and
        what `Text.build`-style construction uses for teletypes: inside `Teletype.Builder().build`,
        `append` adds a `Teletype`, a `Text` (unstyled) or a `Char`, and the result is
        compressed to runs where that helps. Hyperlinks and insertions are shifted to their
        new positions as pieces are appended.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val built: Teletype = Teletype.Builder().build:
          append(e"$Bold(one) ")
          append(t"two ")
          append('3')

        built.plain   // t"one two 3"
        ```
    entry #length   how many characters have been appended so far
  entry #plain   the text, without any styling: exactly the characters a terminal shows
  entry #styles   the style words: one per character plus a trailing one, or one per run
      In the dense form, `styles` has `plain.length + 1` entries, the last being the
      trailing style (the style in force after the final character); in the sparse form it
      has one entry per run in `#boundaries` plus the trailing style. Read it through
      `#styleAt` rather than by index.
  entry #hyperlinks   the OSC 8 hyperlinks, by the character position where each opens
      `e"hi ${Hyperlink(t"https://x.test")}[click]".hyperlinks(3)` is `t"https://x.test"`.
      The `HyperlinkChange` flag in the style word of that position marks where the link
      opens, and the closing one where it ends.
  entry #insertions   raw escape sequences to emit before the character at each position
      What an `Escape(on, off)` substitution leaves behind: the `on` sequence at the start of
      its span and the `off` sequence at its end, each keyed by position and emitted verbatim
      by `#render` on a terminal with ANSI support.
  entry #boundaries   the start position of each style run, or empty for the dense form
      `boundaries(0)` is always `0` when present; run `i` covers from `boundaries(i)` to the
      next boundary or the end of the text. `#isDense` is whether this is empty.
  entry #isDense   whether the styles are stored one per character rather than as runs
  entry #styleAt   the style word of the character at a position, or the trailing style at the length
      `styleAt(p)` for `0 ≤ p ≤ plain.length`: the packed `StyleWord` (as a `Long`) of the
      character at `p`, found by a binary search of the runs in the sparse form, or the
      trailing style when `p` is the text's length. `(teletype.styleAt(0) & StyleWord.Bold) != 0`
      tests whether the first character is bold.
  entry #trailingStyle   the style in force after the last character
      What `#append` continues in, and what a join carries across a boundary. For
      `e"$Bold(abc)def"` it is `0L`, the default, since the bold span closed.
  entry #asSparseArrays   the styles as runs: the `(styles, boundaries)` pair of the sparse form
      Constant time for an already-sparse teletype, and a scan of the characters for a dense
      one. `#append` uses it to merge two teletypes of different forms.
  entry #explicit   the true-colour rendering with every escape character written as `\e`, for inspection
      `e"$Bold(x)".explicit` is `t"\e[1mx\e[22m"` as visible text: the rendering for a
      true-colour xterm, with the control character replaced by the two characters `\` and
      `e`, for tests and debugging output.
  entry #append   this teletype followed by another, or by unstyled text
      `append(that: Teletype)` concatenates both, shifting the second's hyperlinks and
      insertions by the first's length and merging adjacent runs of equal style; `append(text:
      Text)` adds the text in the `#trailingStyle`. Appending an empty operand returns the
      receiver unchanged. `+` is the same, through `.add`.
  entry #dropChars   the teletype without its first `n` characters, or its last with `Rtl`
      `e"abcdef".dropChars(2)` is `e"cdef"`, and `dropChars(2, Rtl)` is `e"abcd"`, each with
      the styles, hyperlinks and insertions of the kept characters moved to their new
      positions. Dropping more than the length gives `Teletype.empty`; dropping a
      non-positive count gives the receiver.
  entry #takeChars   the first `n` characters of the teletype, or its last with `Rtl`
      `e"abcdef".takeChars(3)` is `e"abc"`, and `takeChars(3, Rtl)` is `e"def"`. The trailing
      style of the result is the default. Taking at least the length gives the receiver.
  entry #render   the text with ANSI escape sequences for a terminal, as a `Termcap` describes it
      With `termcap.ansi` false, the result is `#plain`. Otherwise the style words are
      walked run by run and only the *differences* between consecutive styles are emitted,
      as one SGR sequence each (`\e[1;38;2;255;0;0m`), with colours as 24-bit `38;2;r;g;b`
      when `termcap.color` is `ColorDepth.TrueColor` and as the nearest of the 256-colour
      cube (`38;5;n`) otherwise; hyperlinks become OSC 8 sequences and insertions are
      emitted verbatim at their positions. The trailing style is emitted at the end, so a
      teletype which ends inside a span still resets.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      e"${Fg(WebColors.Red)}(x)".render(termcapDefinitions.xtermTrueColorTermcap).contains(t"38;2;255;0;0")
      e"${Fg(WebColors.Red)}(x)".render(termcapDefinitions.xterm256Termcap).contains(t"38;5;")
      e"$Bold(x)".render(termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap)   // t"x"
      ```

entry .e   the `e"…"` interpolator: styled text as a `Teletype`, with its brackets checked as the code compiles
    `e"…"` builds a `Teletype` from literal text and substitutions. A substituted value is
    one of two kinds, decided by the `Insertion[Ansi.Input, T]` given found for its type:

    - a **markup** value — anything with a `Stylize` instance (`Bold`, `Italic`, `Fg(…)`,
      `Bg(…)`, a `Chromatic` colour such as `WebColors.Red`, an `Escape`) or a `Hyperlink` —
      which, when followed immediately by `(`, `[`, `{`, `<` or `«`, styles the span up to
      the matching closing bracket, and otherwise applies to the rest of the enclosing span;
    - a **text** value — a `Teletype`, which keeps its own styling, or any `Teletypeable` or
      `Showable` value, shown as text. A bracket after a text value is ordinary text:
      `e"${42}(text)"` is `42(text)`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import WebColors.{Gold, Indigo, HotPink, White}

    e"$Gold(gold, $Indigo(indigo, $HotPink(hot pink), indigo) $White(and) gold)"
    e"$Bold[bold] and $Italic{italic} and $Underline<underlined> and ${Fg(Gold)}«gold»"
    ```

    Spans nest and restore: each markup substitution transforms the prevailing `TextStyle`
    and the closing bracket restores the style that was in force before it, so the indigo
    above gives way to gold again where it ends. A closing bracket of the wrong kind inside a
    span is ordinary text (`e"$Bold(a]b)"` emboldens `a]b`). A span left unclosed is a compile
    error, _the closing brace does not match an opening brace_; a value with neither a
    `Stylize` nor a `Teletypeable` nor a `Showable` instance is the compile error _can't
    substitute T into an e-interpolator_.

    Two backslashes immediately before a markup substitution cancel it: in
    `e"x\\${Bold}(text)"` the backslashes are consumed, the marker does nothing, and `(text)`
    is literal. The usual text escapes (`\n`, `\t`, `\\`, `\u…`) and `$$` for a literal `$`
    apply as in `t"…"`.

    The interpolator is a macro: it runs the same `Ansi.Runtime` state machine over the
    literal parts at compile time to find bracket errors, and emits a sequence of
    `Ansi.Runtime.parse` and `insert` calls which build the `Teletype` at runtime.

entry .Bold   the marker which emboldens a span: `e"$Bold(text)"` (SGR 1)
    A case object whose `Stylize[Bold.type]` instance (`Ansi.bold`) sets `bold` in the
    prevailing `TextStyle`. On a plain `Termcap` it renders as nothing.

entry .Italic   the marker which italicises a span: `e"$Italic(text)"` (SGR 3)

entry .Underline   the marker which underlines a span: `e"$Underline(text)"` (SGR 4)

entry .Strike   the marker which strikes a span through: `e"$Strike(text)"` (SGR 9)

entry .Reverse   the marker which swaps a span's foreground and background: `e"$Reverse(text)"` (SGR 7)

entry .Conceal   the marker which hides a span's characters: `e"$Conceal(text)"` (SGR 8)
    The characters are still in `Teletype#plain` and still occupy their cells; a terminal
    which honours SGR 8 draws them invisibly.

entry .Faint   the marker which dims a span: `e"$Faint(text)"` (SGR 2)
    Reached as `escapade.Faint`: it is not among the umbrella's exports.

entry .DoubleUnderline   the marker which double-underlines a span: `e"$DoubleUnderline(text)"` (SGR 21)
    Reached as `escapade.DoubleUnderline`: it is not among the umbrella's exports.

entry .BlinkSlow   the marker which makes a span blink slowly: `e"$BlinkSlow(text)"` (SGR 5)
    Reached as `escapade.BlinkSlow`: it is not among the umbrella's exports.

entry .BlinkFast   the marker which makes a span blink rapidly: `e"$BlinkFast(text)"` (SGR 6)
    Reached as `escapade.BlinkFast`: it is not among the umbrella's exports.

entry .Overline   the marker which overlines a span: `e"$Overline(text)"` (SGR 53)
    Reached as `escapade.Overline`: it is not among the umbrella's exports.

entry .Fg   a foreground colour, for `e"${Fg(color)}(text)"` and `Colorable`
  detail
      `Fg(color)` wraps a `Chroma` — a 24-bit colour — as a foreground. `Fg.apply` takes any
      `Chromatic` value (an iridescence `Color in Srgb`, a `WebColors` entry, an `rgb"…"`
      literal, a `Chroma`), so `Fg(WebColors.Red)` and `Fg(Chroma(255, 0, 0))` are the same.
      Its `Stylize` instance (`Ansi.fg`) sets the foreground of the prevailing style for a
      span; a bare `Chromatic` value substituted into `e"…"` does the same through
      `Ansi.chromatic`, so `e"${WebColors.Red}(text)"` needs no `Fg` at all.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val warning: Fg = Fg(WebColors.Orange)
      e"$warning(careful) and ${WebColors.Red}(stop)"
      ```
  refer escapade.Bg
  refer escapade.Colorable
  entry .apply   `Fg(color)` for any `Chromatic` colour
  entry #color   the colour, as a `Chroma`
  entry #bg   the same colour as a background
  entry #ansi   the SGR sequence which sets this foreground, for a colour depth
      `\e[38;2;r;g;bm` for `ColorDepth.TrueColor`, and `\e[38;5;nm` with the nearest entry
      of the 256-colour cube (greys on the 24-step ramp, pure black `16` and white `231`)
      for any other depth. `Teletype#render` emits the same codes.

entry .Bg   a background colour, for `e"${Bg(color)}(text)"`
  detail
      `Bg(color)` wraps a `Chroma` as a background; `Bg.apply` takes any `Chromatic` value.
      Its `Stylize` instance (`Ansi.bg`) sets the background of the prevailing style for a
      span, and `#highContrast` picks the foreground — black or white — which reads best on
      it, which is how `Ribbon` colours its labels.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val highlight: Bg = Bg(WebColors.Yellow)
      e"$highlight(${highlight.highContrast}(marked))"
      ```
  refer escapade.Fg
  refer escapade.Ribbon
  entry .apply   `Bg(color)` for any `Chromatic` colour
  entry #color   the colour, as a `Chroma`
  entry #fg   the same colour as a foreground
  entry #highContrast   black or white as a foreground, whichever contrasts more with this background
      White (`Chroma(0xffffff)`) when the background's luma — `0.21·red + 0.72·green +
      0.07·blue` — is at most 128, and black otherwise. `Bg(Chroma(0, 0, 0)).highContrast` is
      white; `Bg(Chroma(255, 255, 255)).highContrast` is black.
  entry #ansi   the SGR sequence which sets this background, for a colour depth
      `\e[48;2;r;g;bm` in true colour, and `\e[48;5;nm` with the nearest 256-colour cube entry
      otherwise.

entry .TextStyle   the style of one character: its colours and eleven attribute flags, as a record
  detail
      The record a `Stylize` transformation edits. Every field has a default — `Unset`
      colours and `false` flags — so a transformation is written with `copy`:
      `Stylize(_.copy(bold = true, fg = Chroma(0xff0000)))`. The `e"…"` interpolator keeps a
      stack of these as it parses, one pushed per opened span, and packs each into a
      `StyleWord` (`#styleWord`) for the characters it covers.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val alert: TextStyle = TextStyle(fg = Chroma(0xffffff), bg = Chroma(0xcc0000), bold = true)
      Teletype.styled(t"ALERT")(_ => alert)
      ```
  refer escapade.Stylize
  refer escapade.StyleWord
  entry .esc   the escape character, `'\u001b'`
  entry #fg   the foreground colour, or `Unset` for the terminal's default
  entry #bg   the background colour, or `Unset` for the terminal's default
  entry #italic   whether the text is italic
  entry #bold   whether the text is bold
  entry #reverse   whether foreground and background are swapped
  entry #underline   whether the text is underlined
  entry #conceal   whether the text is hidden
  entry #strike   whether the text is struck through
  entry #faint   whether the text is dimmed
  entry #doubleUnderline   whether the text is double-underlined
  entry #blinkSlow   whether the text blinks slowly
  entry #blinkFast   whether the text blinks rapidly
  entry #overline   whether the text is overlined
  entry #styleWord   the style packed into a `Long`, as `Teletype#styles` holds it
      The foreground's 24 bits in the low bytes with the `FgSet` flag, the background's 24
      bits above them with `BgSet`, and one bit per attribute: the layout `StyleWord`
      documents. `TextStyle().styleWord` is `0L`, the default.
  entry #addChanges   appends to a buffer the SGR sequence which turns this style into another
      `addChanges(buffer, next, colorDepth)` emits only the differences between this style
      and `next`, as `StyleWord.emitDiff` does; `Teletype#render` works the same way, run by
      run.

entry .Stylize   the typeclass which makes a type usable as markup in `e"…"`: a transformation of the prevailing style
  detail
      `Stylize[T]` is `Substitution[Ansi.Input, T, "esc"]`: an `Insertion` whose label `esc`
      tells the interpolator that a `T` substituted before a bracket opens a styled span.
      An instance is written with `Stylize(lambda)`, where the lambda maps the `TextStyle` in
      force to the style of the span — a transformation rather than an absolute setting, so
      a marker can depend on what it finds:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case object Toggle
      given Stylize[Toggle.type] = _ => Stylize(style => style.copy(bold = !style.bold))

      e"$Toggle(bold) $Bold($Toggle(not bold))"
      ```

      The given is written as a function from the value to the markup (`_ => Stylize(…)`), so a
      value's fields can inform the style, as `Ansi.fg` reads the colour out of an `Fg`. The
      instances for the eleven markers, `Fg`, `Bg`, every `Chromatic` colour and `Escape` live
      in `Ansi`'s companion, and a `Hyperlink` is a substitution of the same label which opens
      an OSC 8 link rather than a style.

      `Stylize(lambda)` itself returns the `Ansi.Input.Markup` which the interpolator consumes.
  refer escapade.TextStyle
  refer escapade.Ansi
  entry .apply   `Stylize(lambda)`: the markup which applies a `TextStyle -> TextStyle` transformation to a span
      Wraps the lambda as an `Ansi.Input.Markup`, the value a `Stylize[T]` instance returns
      for each substituted `T`. The lambda receives the style in force where the span opens,
      and its result styles the span's characters.

entry .Escape   a raw pair of escape sequences to wrap a span in: `Escape(on, off)`
  detail
      `Escape` is the type `Ansi.Input.Escape`, with `Escape(code, reset)` to build one: the
      text after the escape character which switches something on, and the text which switches
      it off again (empty by default). Substituted into `e"…"` before a bracket, the `on`
      sequence is inserted where the span opens and the `off` sequence where it closes, both
      emitted verbatim by `Teletype#render` on a terminal with ANSI support and dropped on a
      plain one; without a bracket, only `on` is inserted. The `escapes` object has the
      standard SGR pairs ready made.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import termcapDefinitions.xtermTrueColorTermcap

      val bold: Escape = Escape(t"[1m", t"[22m")
      e"a$bold(b)c".render(xtermTrueColorTermcap)   // t"a\e[1mb\e[22mc"
      ```

      Unlike a `Stylize` marker, an `Escape` is opaque to the style stack: it does not update
      the `TextStyle`, so `Teletype#styleAt` does not know about it and a nested style span
      does not restore it.
  refer escapade.escapes
  entry .apply   `Escape(code, reset)`: an escape pair from the text after each escape character
      `code` is inserted where the span opens and `reset`, which defaults to `Unset` (no
      sequence), where it closes; both are written without the leading escape character,
      which the `e"…"` parser adds as it records the insertion.

entry .Hyperlink   a URL to attach to a span, as an OSC 8 terminal hyperlink: `e"${Hyperlink(url)}[text]"`
  detail
      Substituted before a bracket, the span becomes a clickable link on terminals which
      support OSC 8: `#render` emits `\e]8;;url\e\\` where it opens and `\e]8;;\e\\` where it
      closes, and a plain `Termcap` shows just the text. The link's position is kept in
      `Teletype#hyperlinks`, and a styled span inside a link keeps both.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val link = e"see ${Hyperlink(t"https://example.com")}[the documentation] for more"
      link.plain   // t"see the documentation for more"
      ```
  entry #url   the link's target, as text

entry .Ansi   the machinery behind `e"…"`: the `Stylize` instances, the parser state and its runtime
  detail
      `Ansi` holds everything the `e"…"` interpolator is built from. Its companion carries the
      `Stylize` givens for the markers and colours (so they resolve with no import), the
      `Input` a substitution becomes, the `State` the parser accumulates and the `Runtime`
      which drives it, and `Ansi.Error`, the exception a malformed interpolation raises. A
      program styling text through `e"…"` need not touch any of it; code which generates
      teletypes without the interpolator may drive `Runtime` directly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val state = Ansi.Runtime.parse(Ansi.Runtime.initial, t"plain ")
      Ansi.Runtime.insert(state, Ansi.Input.Markup(_.copy(bold = true)))
      Ansi.Runtime.parse(state, t"(bold) tail")
      Ansi.Runtime.complete(state).plain   // t"plain bold tail"
      ```

      `Ansi.strip(text)` removes every ANSI escape sequence from text; `unstyled` (in
      `escapade.csi`) is the stricter extension on `Text`, which matches only complete CSI
      sequences.
  refer escapade.e
  refer escapade.Stylize
  refer escapade.Teletype
  entry .Transform   a `TextStyle -> TextStyle` function: what a `Stylize` instance produces
  entry .strip   the text with every ANSI escape sequence removed
      Matches `\e[` followed by parameters and a final byte in `@`–`~`, and deletes each
      match, leaving the characters that would have been printed. The `unstyled` extension
      is usually preferable: it matches only complete CSI sequences (`csi.pattern`).
  entry .escape   the `Stylize` instance for `Escape`: the raw pair is inserted around the span
  entry .chromatic   the `Stylize` instance for any `Chromatic` colour: the span's foreground
      `e"${WebColors.Red}(text)"` colours the text red with no `Fg` wrapper; an `rgb"…"` literal
      or a `Chroma` works the same way.
  entry .bg   the `Stylize` instance for `Bg`: the span's background
  entry .fg   the `Stylize` instance for `Fg`: the span's foreground
  entry .bold   the `Stylize` instance for `Bold`
  entry .italic   the `Stylize` instance for `Italic`
  entry .underline   the `Stylize` instance for `Underline`
  entry .strike   the `Stylize` instance for `Strike`
  entry .conceal   the `Stylize` instance for `Conceal`
  entry .reverse   the `Stylize` instance for `Reverse`
  entry .faint   the `Stylize` instance for `Faint`
  entry .doubleUnderline   the `Stylize` instance for `DoubleUnderline`
  entry .blinkSlow   the `Stylize` instance for `BlinkSlow`
  entry .blinkFast   the `Stylize` instance for `BlinkFast`
  entry .overline   the `Stylize` instance for `Overline`
  entry .hyperlink   the substitution for `Hyperlink`: the span becomes an OSC 8 link
      Labelled `esc` like a `Stylize`, so a bracket after it opens a span, but the span is a
      link frame rather than a style frame.
  entry .Input   what a substitution into `e"…"` becomes: text, markup, a raw escape pair or a hyperlink
      An enum of four cases. `TextInput(text: Teletype)` is a value shown as text, appended
      with its styling combined under the prevailing style; `Markup(transform: Transform)` is
      a `Stylize` value, pending until the next character decides whether it opens a span;
      `Escape(on: Text, off: Text)` — the type the package alias `Escape` names — is a raw
      pair; `Hyperlink(url: Text)` opens a link. `Runtime.insert` feeds one to a `State`.
  entry .Pending   a markup, link or escape substitution waiting to see whether a bracket follows it
      The parser's one-token lookahead, as an enum: `Style(transform)`, `Link(url)` or
      `Escape(on, off)`. The next character resolves it — an opening bracket pushes a frame,
      a backslash cancels it, and anything else applies it to the rest of the enclosing span.
  entry .Frame   an open span on the parser's stack, with the bracket which will close it
    detail
        An enum: `Style(bracket)` for a styled span, `Link(bracket)` for a hyperlink and
        `Escape(bracket, off)` for a raw escape pair, which remembers the `off` sequence to
        insert when the span closes. Each knows the closing bracket it waits for, so a
        closing bracket of another kind is ordinary text.
    entry #bracket   the closing bracket which ends this span: `)`, `]`, `}`, `>` or `»`
  entry .State   the mutable accumulator the interpolator fills: text, styles, links, insertions and the open spans
    detail
        One `State` is created per interpolation by `Runtime.initial`, fed by `Runtime.parse`
        and `Runtime.insert`, and turned into a `Teletype` by `Runtime.complete`. It is
        mutable and single-use: a program building teletypes by hand should prefer
        `Teletype.Builder`, which is the same accumulator without the bracket parsing.
    entry #plain   the characters so far
    entry #styles   the style word of each character so far
    entry #hyperlinks   the hyperlinks opened so far, by position
    entry #insertions   the raw escape sequences so far, by position
    entry #last   the substitution awaiting its bracket, if any
    entry #stack   the open spans, innermost first
    entry #styleStack   the styles in force outside each open style span, innermost first
    entry #currentStyle   the style the next character will take
    entry #linkArmed   whether the next character opens or closes a hyperlink
    entry #appendChar   adds one character in the current style
    entry #appendChars   adds text, every character in the current style
    entry #appendTeletype   adds a teletype, each character's own style combined under the current one
        An inner style wins where it sets a colour, and attribute flags from both are kept,
        as `StyleWord.combine` defines; the teletype's hyperlinks and insertions are shifted
        to their new positions.
    entry #addInsertion   records a raw escape sequence to emit before the character at a position
    entry #pushStyleFrame   opens a styled span: saves the current style and applies a transformation
    entry #pushLinkFrame   opens a hyperlink span at the current position
    entry #pushEscapeFrame   opens a raw escape span, inserting its `on` sequence
    entry #popFrame   closes the innermost span, restoring the style or inserting the `off` sequence
    entry #applyOnce   applies a transformation to the current style with no span to close it
  entry .Runtime   the parser: feeds literal parts and substitutions to a `State`, and completes it
    detail
        The functions the `e"…"` macro emits calls to, and which it also runs at compile time
        over the literal parts to find bracket errors. Each takes the `State`, mutates it and
        returns it, so calls chain.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val state = Ansi.Runtime.initial
        Ansi.Runtime.parse(state, t"a ")
        Ansi.Runtime.insert(state, Ansi.Input.Hyperlink(t"https://x.test"))
        Ansi.Runtime.parse(state, t"[link] b")
        Ansi.Runtime.complete(state).hyperlinks(2)   // t"https://x.test"
        ```
    entry .initial   a fresh, empty `State`
    entry .parse   feeds a literal part of the interpolation to the state
        Resolves any pending substitution against the part's first character (an opening
        bracket pushes a frame; a backslash cancels; anything else applies once), then scans
        for the innermost frame's closing bracket, appending text up to it and popping the
        frame, repeatedly, with the text escapes (`\n`, `\t`, `\u…`) decoded. A bad escape
        raises `Ansi.Error`.
    entry .insert   feeds a substitution to the state
        A `TextInput` is appended at once; `Markup`, `Escape` and `Hyperlink` become the
        pending substitution which the next `parse` resolves.
    entry .skip   feeds an empty text input, which resolves nothing and appends nothing
        What the macro emits for a substitution whose markup was cancelled by a preceding
        `\\`.
    entry .complete   the finished `Teletype`, or `Ansi.Error` if a span is still open
        Raises `Ansi.Error`: _the closing brace does not match an opening brace_ when the
        frame stack is not empty; otherwise appends the trailing style, compresses the styles
        to runs where that helps, and returns the teletype.
  entry .Error   the exception for a malformed interpolation: an unclosed span or a bad text escape
    detail
        An `Exception` whose message is `escapade: ` followed by `#detail`. The `e"…"` macro
        runs the parser at compile time and reports the same detail as a compile error, so
        at runtime it arises only from driving `Runtime` directly.
    entry #detail   what was wrong, as a `Message`
  entry .Escapes   how each style attribute is spelled as an escape sequence: the trait, and the `Standard` SGR spelling
    detail
        A `Findable` trait with one method per attribute, returning the sequence which switches
        it on or off, and one each for a 24-bit foreground and background. `Escapes.Standard`
        is the ordinary SGR spelling (`\e[1m` and `\e[22m` for bold, `\e[38;2;r;g;bm` for a
        foreground). `Teletype2#render` takes one as a contextual value; `Teletype#render`
        spells the sequences itself from the `Termcap`.
    entry .Standard   the standard SGR sequences: `\e[1m`/`\e[22m`, `\e[3m`/`\e[23m`, and so on
    entry #bold   the sequence which switches bold on or off
    entry #italic   the sequence which switches italic on or off
    entry #underline   the sequence which switches underline on or off
    entry #reverse   the sequence which switches reverse video on or off
    entry #conceal   the sequence which switches concealment on or off
    entry #strike   the sequence which switches strikethrough on or off
    entry #foreground   the sequence which sets a 24-bit foreground colour
    entry #background   the sequence which sets a 24-bit background colour

entry .Ansi2   the lower-priority parent of `Ansi`, holding the text-substitution given
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `Ansi` extends it so that `Ansi.teletype`, the substitution which
      admits any `Teletypeable` or `Showable` value as text, ranks below the markup
      substitutions declared in `Ansi` itself. A value is shown through its `Teletypeable`
      if it has one, and through its `Showable` otherwise.
  entry #TeletypeSubstitution   a `Substitution` into `e"…"` of label `t`, from a function to `Teletype`
    entry #embed   wraps a value as `Ansi.Input.TextInput` of its `Teletype` rendering
  entry #teletype   admits any `Teletypeable`, else `Showable`, value into `e"…"` as text
      Inherited by `Ansi`, so it resolves as `Ansi.teletype` from implicit scope with no
      import, below every markup substitution.

entry .Teletypeable   the typeclass of values which render as a `Teletype`: styled, where `Showable` is plain
  detail
      `value.teletype` renders a value as styled text, as `value.show` renders it as plain
      text. The instances in the companion cover `Teletype` itself, `Text`, a `Message` (its
      nested quotations in yellow italics), an `Option`, a `Double` (in yellow, through the
      `Decimalizer` in scope), an `Error` (its message) and a `Throwable` (its simple class
      name in red), and every `Showable` value falls back to its plain rendering; a type with
      a `Colorable` instance renders in that colour.

      A `Teletypeable` is what the `e"…"` interpolator and the `Teletype.textual` instance
      use to show a substituted value, so giving a type an instance styles it wherever it
      is interpolated:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Status(ok: Boolean, text: Text)

      given Status is Teletypeable = status =>
        if status.ok then e"${Fg(WebColors.Green)}(✓ ${status.text})"
        else e"${Fg(WebColors.Red)}(✗ ${status.text})"

      e"build: ${Status(true, t"passed")}"
      ```

      Other libraries add instances in `teletypeables` packages — a `Graphical` image here,
      exceptions and stack traces in digression, URLs in urticose — imported by name.
  refer escapade.teletype
  refer escapade.Colorable
  refer escapade.teletypeables
  entry .teletype   a `Teletype` is its own rendering
  entry .text   a `Text` renders unstyled
  entry .message   a `Message` renders with its quoted parts in italic yellow, and nested quotes bold too
      The message is folded level by level: plain text at level 0, level-1 quotations
      (`m"the file $path"`'s `$path`) in italic `Chroma(0xefe68b)`, and deeper nesting in
      bold italic `Chroma(0xffd600)`.
  entry .option   an `Option` renders its value, or the text `empty`
  entry .colorable   a value with both `Showable` and `Colorable` instances renders in its colour
      One prioritised given rather than two peers: as two, a type with both upstream
      instances matched both, ambiguously. This one prefers `Colorable`,
      and `Teletypeable2#showable` (a lower-priority owner) is the plain fallback.
  entry .error   an `Error` renders as its `Message`
  entry .double   a `Double` renders in yellow, formatted by the `Decimalizer` in scope
  entry .throwable   a `Throwable` renders as its simple class name, in red
      The run of characters after the last `.` of the class's binary name, in
      `Chroma(0xdc133b)`; digression's `teletypeables` has the full stack-trace rendering.
  entry #teletype   the rendering of a value
  entry #contramap   an instance for another type, by a function into this one
      `summon[Text is Teletypeable].contramap[Int](_.show)` renders an `Int` through the
      text instance.

entry .Teletypeable2   the lower-priority owner of the plain `Showable` fallback for `Teletypeable`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `Teletypeable`'s companion extends it so that `#showable` ranks
      below `Teletypeable.colorable`.
  entry #showable   any `Showable` value renders unstyled, as `Teletype(value.show)`

entry .teletype   `value.teletype`: a value rendered as a `Teletype` through its `Teletypeable` instance
    An extension on any value with a `Teletypeable` instance. `42.teletype` renders through
    the `Showable` fallback, `m"…".teletype` in the message style, and a value with a
    `Colorable` instance in its colour.

entry .Colorable   the typeclass which assigns a value a foreground colour, so it renders in it
  detail
      A type with a `Colorable` instance renders through `Teletypeable.colorable` as its
      `show` text in the colour `#color` gives, with no `Teletypeable` of its own. The
      colour may be fixed for the type or chosen per value:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Fruit(name: Text)
      given Fruit is Showable = _.name
      given Fruit is Colorable = Colorable(WebColors.Red)

      Fruit(t"cherry").teletype   // cherry, in red

      enum Level:
        case Info, Warn
      given Log.Level is Showable = _.toString.tt

      given Log.Level is Colorable = Colorable[Log.Level]: level => level match
          case Log.Level.Info => WebColors.SteelBlue
          case Log.Level.Warn => WebColors.Orange
      ```
  refer escapade.Teletypeable
  refer escapade.Fg
  entry .apply   an instance from a fixed `Chromatic` colour, or from a function choosing one per value
      `Colorable[T](color)` colours every `T` the same; `Colorable[T](chooseColor)` takes a
      `T -> color` for any `Chromatic` colour type. The type parameter is given explicitly in
      the second form, since the function's parameter type does not infer from the result.
  entry #color   the value's colour, as an `Fg`

entry .Imprintable   the typeclass of content which decomposes into grapheme cells, each with a `StyleWord`
  detail
      A terminal surface places content cell by cell, and a cell holds a *grapheme* — a
      wide CJK glyph or a ZWJ emoji is one cell, a combining mark adds none — not a `Char`.
      `cells(content)(lambda)` calls `lambda` once per grapheme with the style word that
      styles it: the default style for `Text`, `Ascii` and `Writing`, and for a `Teletype`
      the style of the grapheme's first character, so colour survives decomposition. The
      instances are in the companion, so any of these types imprints without an import.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      var cells: List[(Grapheme, StyleWord)] = Nil

      summon[Teletype is Imprintable].cells(e"a$Bold(b)c"): (grapheme, style) =>
        cells = (grapheme, style) :: cells

      cells.reverse.map(_(1).isBold)   // List(false, true, false)
      ```
  refer escapade.StyleWord
  entry .text   `Text` imprints one cell per grapheme, in the default style
  entry .writing   a hieroglyph `Writing` imprints its graphemes in the default style
  entry .ascii   an `Ascii` text imprints one cell per character, in the default style
  entry .teletype   a `Teletype` imprints each grapheme with the style of its first character
  entry #cells   calls a function for each grapheme cell of the content, with its style word

entry .Ribbon   a row of coloured labels with arrow-shaped joins, as a status line or breadcrumb
  detail
      `Ribbon(colors*)` takes background colours, and `#fill(parts*)` lays each part on the
      corresponding background — in `Bg#highContrast` black or white — followed by the powerline arrow glyph (U+E0B0)
      in that background's colour, so each segment appears to point into the next, in the
      manner of a powerline prompt. The glyph needs a font with the powerline symbols. With
      no colours, the parts are joined with a space.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val ribbon = Ribbon(WebColors.SteelBlue, WebColors.SeaGreen, WebColors.Goldenrod)
      ribbon.fill(e"main", e"lib/escapade", e"3 changed")
      ```
  refer escapade.Bg
  entry .apply   `Ribbon(colors*)` from any `Chromatic` colours, each wrapped as a `Bg`
  entry #colors   the backgrounds, one per segment
  entry #fill   the parts laid on the backgrounds in order, joined with arrow glyphs
      Parts beyond the colours are dropped (the pairs are zipped), and the last segment's
      arrow points into the terminal's default background.

entry .escapes   the raw SGR sequences of the sixteen-colour terminal, as `Escape` pairs
  detail
      The classic terminal's colours and attributes, each an `Escape(on, off)` pair for a
      span in `e"…"`, for a program which wants the terminal's *own* palette (`escapes.foreground.Red`
      is whatever the user's theme makes red) rather than a `Chroma` it chooses itself. A
      `Fg` or `Bg` with a 24-bit colour is the usual choice; these are for matching the
      palette of the surrounding shell.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      e"${escapes.foreground.Red}(error) ${escapes.styles.Bold}(bold) ${escapes.background.Blue}(on blue)"
      ```
  refer escapade.Escape
  entry .foreground   the sixteen foreground colours, SGR 30–37 and 90–97, each reset by SGR 39
    detail
        `Black`, `Red`, `Green`, `Yellow`, `Blue`, `Magenta`, `Cyan` and `White` are SGR 30
        to 37; the `Bright…` eight are SGR 90 to 97. Each closes with `[39m`, the default
        foreground.
    entry .Black   SGR 30
    entry .Red   SGR 31
    entry .Green   SGR 32
    entry .Yellow   SGR 33
    entry .Blue   SGR 34
    entry .Magenta   SGR 35
    entry .Cyan   SGR 36
    entry .White   SGR 37
    entry .BrightBlack   SGR 90
    entry .BrightRed   SGR 91
    entry .BrightGreen   SGR 92
    entry .BrightYellow   SGR 93
    entry .BrightBlue   SGR 94
    entry .BrightMagenta   SGR 95
    entry .BrightCyan   SGR 96
    entry .BrightWhite   SGR 97, though its reset is `[49m` (the background default) rather than `[39m`
  entry .background   the sixteen background colours, SGR 40–47 and 100–107, each reset by SGR 49
    detail
        `Black` to `White` are SGR 40 to 47; the `Bright…` eight are SGR 100 to 107. Each
        closes with `[49m`, the default background.
    entry .Black   SGR 40
    entry .Red   SGR 41
    entry .Green   SGR 42
    entry .Yellow   SGR 43
    entry .Blue   SGR 44
    entry .Magenta   SGR 45
    entry .Cyan   SGR 46
    entry .White   SGR 47
    entry .BrightBlack   SGR 100
    entry .BrightRed   SGR 101
    entry .BrightGreen   SGR 102
    entry .BrightYellow   SGR 103
    entry .BrightBlue   SGR 104
    entry .BrightMagenta   SGR 105
    entry .BrightCyan   SGR 106
    entry .BrightWhite   SGR 107
  entry .styles   the attribute pairs: bold, light, italic, underline, blink, reverse, conceal, strike
    detail
        Each is the SGR code which switches the attribute on, paired with the one which
        switches it off: `Bold` is `[1m`/`[22m`, `Light` (faint) `[2m`/`[22m`, `Italic`
        `[3m`/`[23m`, `Underline` `[4m`/`[24m`, `SlowBlink` `[5m`/`[25m`, `FastBlink`
        `[6m`/`[25m`, `Reverse` `[7m`/`[27m`, `Conceal` `[8m`/`[28m` and `Strike` `[9m`/`[29m`.
        The markers `Bold`, `Italic` and the rest do the same through the style stack, and
        are the usual choice.
    entry .Bold   SGR 1, reset by 22
    entry .Light   SGR 2 (faint), reset by 22
    entry .Italic   SGR 3, reset by 23
    entry .Underline   SGR 4, reset by 24
    entry .SlowBlink   SGR 5, reset by 25
    entry .FastBlink   SGR 6, reset by 25
    entry .Reverse   SGR 7, reset by 27
    entry .Conceal   SGR 8, reset by 28
    entry .Strike   SGR 9, reset by 29
  entry .Reset   SGR 0: every attribute and colour back to the default
  entry .EraseLine   `[0K`: clears from the cursor to the end of the line
      The `off` sequence is `[0m`, so used as a span it also resets styling where it closes.
  entry .title   the OSC 0 sequence which sets the terminal window's title
      `escapes.title(t"build")` is an `Escape` with no reset, so it is substituted without a
      bracket: `e"${escapes.title(t"build")}"`.
  entry .link   text wrapped in an OSC 8 hyperlink, as raw `Text`
      `escapes.link(url, text)` takes any URL type `Abstractable across Urls to Text` and
      returns the text with the link sequences around it, already rendered. For a link inside
      a `Teletype`, use `Hyperlink`, which stays a value until rendering.

entry .csi   the ANSI control sequences — cursor movement, erasure, scrolling, SGR — spelled as `Text`
  detail
      `csi` is the Control Sequence Introducer, `\e[`, and this object spells each standard
      sequence by its ECMA-48 mnemonic: `csi.cuu(3)` moves the cursor up three lines,
      `csi.el()` erases the line, `csi.sgr(1, 31)` sets bold red. Each result is plain `Text`
      containing the control characters, to be written to a terminal directly; a `Teletype`
      renders its own styling and does not need these. The `escapade.csi` source set holds
      this object alone (with `unstyled`), depending only on gossamer, so a library which
      moves the cursor need not depend on the rest of escapade.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      csi.cuu()          // t"\e[A"
      csi.cup(2, 5)      // t"\e[2;5H"
      csi.sgr(1, 31)     // t"\e[1;31m"
      csi.dectcem(false) // t"\e[?25l": hide the cursor
      ```

      A count parameter is `Optional`: `Unset` (the default) omits it, which the terminal
      reads as `1`.
  refer escapade.unstyled
  entry .esc   the escape character, as text: `\e`
  entry .csi   the Control Sequence Introducer: `\e[`
  entry .pattern   a regex matching one control sequence: `\e[`, parameters, and a final letter
      Every CSI sequence, not only SGR, so cursor movement and erasure match too. `unstyled`
      deletes its matches.
  entry .cuu   cursor up `n` lines (CUU, final byte `A`)
  entry .cud   cursor down `n` lines (CUD, `B`)
  entry .cuf   cursor forward `n` columns (CUF, `C`)
  entry .cub   cursor back `n` columns (CUB, `D`)
  entry .cnl   cursor to the start of the line `n` lines down (CNL, `E`)
  entry .cpl   cursor to the start of the line `n` lines up (CPL, `F`)
  entry .cha   cursor to column `n` of the current line (CHA, `G`)
  entry .cup   cursor to row `n`, column `m` (CUP, `H`); both default to `1`
  entry .ed   erase in display (ED, `J`): `0` to the end, `1` to the start, `2` all, `3` scrollback too
  entry .el   erase in line (EL, `K`): `0` to the end of the line, `1` to its start, `2` the whole line
  entry .su   scroll the page up `n` lines (SU, `S`)
  entry .sd   scroll the page down `n` lines (SD, `T`)
  entry .hvp   horizontal and vertical position (HVP, `f`): the same as `cup`, with both arguments required
  entry .dsr   device status report (DSR, `6n`): asks the terminal to report the cursor position
  entry .scp   save the cursor position (SCP, `s`)
  entry .rcp   restore the saved cursor position (RCP, `u`)
  entry .sgr   select graphic rendition: `csi.sgr(1, 31)` is `\e[1;31m`
  entry .dectcem   show (`true`) or hide (`false`, the default) the cursor: `\e[?25h` or `\e[?25l`

entry .unstyled   `text.unstyled`: the text with every ANSI control sequence removed
    An extension on `Text`, in `escapade.csi`, which deletes every match of `csi.pattern`,
    leaving the characters that would have been printed had the escapes been honoured — all
    CSI sequences, not just the SGR ones, so a cursor movement or an erase-line emitted into
    a captured stream vanishes with the styling. It is the inverse direction to `Teletype`:
    `Teletype#plain` reads the content of a teletype built structurally, where `unstyled`
    starts from text in which the escapes are already characters, such as a subprocess's
    output.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"\e[1mbold\e[0m text".unstyled      // t"bold text"
    t"a\e[2Kb\e[10;20Hc".unstyled         // t"abc"
    t"xs[0m1]".unstyled                   // t"xs[0m1]": a bare bracket is not an escape
    ```

entry .displayableTypes   givens which let more types be printed to `Out` and `Err`, imported by name
  detail
      A choice package, merged with the umbrella's `displayableTypes`: `import
      displayableTypes.ansiMessagePrintable` lets `Out.println(m"…")` print a `Message` with
      its quotations styled.
  entry .ansiMessagePrintable   a `Message` is `Printable`, rendered through `Teletypeable.message`
      `Out.println(m"the file $path was not found")` styles the quoted part in italic yellow
      on a terminal, and prints plainly where the `Termcap` has no ANSI.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import displayableTypes.ansiMessagePrintable

      Out.println(m"the file ${t"build.mill"} was not found")
      ```

entry .writables   streams of `Teletype` records written to the standard output and error, imported by name
  detail
      A choice package in `escapade.io`, merged with the umbrella's `writables`. A stream of
      teletypes travels on the boxed medium (windows of `Array[Teletype]`), and each record
      prints through `Out.print` or `Err.print` as it arrives, rendered by the `Stdio`'s
      `Termcap`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import writables.outTeletypeWritable

      val lines: Stream[Teletype] = Stream(e"$Bold(one)", e"two")
      lines.writeTo(Out)
      ```
  entry .outTeletypeWritable   `Out` is `Writable` by teletype records, each printed as it arrives; needs a `Stdio`
  entry .errTeletypeWritable   `Err` is `Writable` by teletype records, each printed as it arrives; needs a `Stdio`

entry .teletypeables   `Teletypeable` instances for types from other libraries, imported by name
  detail
      A choice package, merged with the umbrella's `teletypeables`, which other libraries
      extend with their own (`exceptionTeletype`, `urlTeletype`, `juxtapositionTeletype`).
      Escapade's own member, in `escapade.graphics`, renders any image.
  entry .graphicalTeletype   any `Graphical` value renders as a `Teletype` of half-block characters, two pixels per cell
      Each terminal cell is an upper half-block `▀` whose foreground is the pixel of one row
      and whose background is the pixel of the row below, so an image of height `h` takes
      `h/2` lines of its width in cells, with a newline after each. The bottom row of an
      odd-height image is dropped.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import teletypeables.graphicalTeletype

      def show[image: Graphical](image: image): Unit = Out.println(image.teletype)
      ```

entry .StyleWord   the style of one character packed into a `Long`: two 24-bit colours and twelve flag bits
    `StyleWord` is the opaque type `escapade.internal.StyleWord`, exported here. The low 24
    bits hold the foreground's RGB and bits 24 to 47 the background's; bit 48 (`FgSet`) and
    bit 49 (`BgSet`) say whether each is set, and bits 50 to 61 are `Bold`, `Faint`,
    `Italic`, `Underline`, `DoubleUnderline`, `BlinkSlow`, `BlinkFast`, `Reverse`, `Conceal`,
    `Strike`, `Overline` and `HyperlinkChange`, each a `Long` constant on the companion for
    masking. `StyleWord.Default` is `0L`, `StyleWord(raw)` wraps a `Long`, and
    `TextStyle#styleWord` packs a record into one.

    Because it erases to `Long`, an `Array[StyleWord]` is an unboxed `long[]`; the companion's
    `classTag` given lets a cell grid allocate one. The extension methods read it: `raw`,
    `fgRgb`, `bgRgb`, `hasFg`, `hasBg`, `isBold`, `isFaint`, `isItalic`, `isUnderline`,
    `isDoubleUnderline`, `isBlinkSlow`, `isBlinkFast`, `isReverse`, `isConceal`, `isStrike`,
    `isOverline` and `hasHyperlinkChange`; and edit it: `withFg(rgb)`, `withBg(rgb)`,
    `clearFg`, `clearBg`, `withBit(bit)`, `withoutBit(bit)` and `applyTransform(mask, bits)`.
    `StyleWord.combine(outer, inner)` is how a nested teletype's style merges under the
    enclosing one — the inner colour wins where set, and the flags of both are kept — and
    `StyleWord.emitDiff(buffer, prev, next, depth)` writes the SGR sequence which turns one
    style into another, which is what `Teletype#render` emits between runs.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val word = e"$Bold(abc)def".styleAt(0)
    (word & StyleWord.Bold) != 0          // true
    StyleWord(word).isBold                // true
    ```

    Reached as `escapade.StyleWord`: it is not among the umbrella's exports.

entry .CharSpan   a half-open range of character positions packed into a `Long`
    `CharSpan` is the opaque type `escapade.internal.CharSpan`, exported here.
    `CharSpan(start, end)` packs the two `Int`s into one `Long`, ordered so that spans
    compare (`CharSpan.comparable`) by start first; `CharSpan.Nowhere` is the empty span at
    `Int.MaxValue`. The extensions `start`, `end` and `nil` read it, and `trimLeft(n)`,
    `takeLeft(n)` and `shift(n)` cut or move it, each returning `Nowhere` when nothing is
    left. It is a building block for code which tracks styled regions by position; the
    `Teletype` representation itself uses run boundaries instead.

entry .internal   the `e"…"` macro and the packed representations behind `Teletype`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macro and the opaque types have a
      stable home. `CharSpan` and `StyleWord` are exported from here to the `escapade`
      package, and their pages there have the full account.
  entry .eMacro   the macro behind `e"…"`: checks the brackets at compile time and emits the runtime parse
      Reads the literal parts, summons an `Insertion[Ansi.Input, T]` for each substitution's
      type, runs `Ansi.Runtime` over the parts at compile time so that an unclosed span is a
      compile error, and splices the equivalent chain of `parse` and `insert` calls ending in
      `complete`. A `\\` before a markup substitution is consumed and the markup skipped.
  entry .CharSpan   a half-open range of positions in a `Long`; see `escapade.CharSpan`
    detail
        `CharSpan(start, end)`, `CharSpan.Nowhere` and the `comparable` given.
    entry .apply   `CharSpan(start, end)`
    entry .comparable   spans order as their packed `Long`s: by start, then by the later end first
    entry .Nowhere   the empty span at `Int.MaxValue`
  entry .start   the first position of a `CharSpan`
  entry .end   the position after the last of a `CharSpan`
  entry .nil   whether a `CharSpan` is empty
  entry .trimLeft   a `CharSpan` with `n` positions removed from the front of its frame
  entry .takeLeft   a `CharSpan` cut to the first `n` positions of its frame
  entry .shift   a `CharSpan` moved `n` positions to the right
  entry .AnsiStyle   a style as a 64-bit set: for each attribute a *set* bit and a *value* bit, plus two 24-bit colours
      The representation `Teletype2` uses, as a hypotenuse `B64`. Each of bold, italic,
      underline, strike and concealed has a bit saying whether this style sets it and a bit
      saying to what, so that `update(changes)` can apply only the attributes a change
      mentions; `indexes` names the bit positions.
  entry .indexes   the bit positions of an `AnsiStyle`'s flags and colour fields, as `Interval`s and masks
    detail
        Each attribute has a `Set…` position, saying whether a style mentions it, and the
        position after it for its value; the colours are 24-bit `Interval`s, each preceded by
        its `Set…` bit. The masks select groups of bits for `update`.
    entry .SetBold   the bit which says a style sets bold
    entry .Bold   the bit holding the bold value
    entry .SetItalic   the bit which says a style sets italic
    entry .Italic   the bit holding the italic value
    entry .SetUnderline   the bit which says a style sets underline
    entry .Underline   the bit holding the underline value
    entry .SetStrike   the bit which says a style sets strikethrough
    entry .Strike   the bit holding the strikethrough value
    entry .SetConcealed   the bit which says a style sets concealment
    entry .Concealed   the bit holding the concealment value
    entry .SetForeground   the bit which says a style sets the foreground
    entry .Foreground   the 24 bits of the foreground colour
    entry .SetBackground   the bit which says a style sets the background
    entry .Background   the 24 bits of the background colour
    entry .Changes   every bit which says a style sets something
    entry .FlagChanges   the `Set…` bits of the five attribute flags
    entry .Mask   the value bits of the five attribute flags
    entry .FgMask   the foreground colour's bits
    entry .BgMask   the background colour's bits
  entry .setBold   whether an `AnsiStyle` sets bold
  entry .bold   the bold value an `AnsiStyle` sets
  entry .setItalic   whether an `AnsiStyle` sets italic
  entry .italic   the italic value an `AnsiStyle` sets
  entry .setUnderline   whether an `AnsiStyle` sets underline
  entry .underline   the underline value an `AnsiStyle` sets
  entry .setStrike   whether an `AnsiStyle` sets strikethrough
  entry .strike   the strikethrough value an `AnsiStyle` sets
  entry .setConcealed   whether an `AnsiStyle` sets concealment
  entry .concealed   the concealment value an `AnsiStyle` sets
  entry .setForeground   whether an `AnsiStyle` sets the foreground
  entry .foreground   the foreground an `AnsiStyle` sets, as a `Chroma`
  entry .setBackground   whether an `AnsiStyle` sets the background
  entry .background   the background an `AnsiStyle` sets, as a `Chroma`
  entry .changed   whether an `AnsiStyle` sets anything at all
  entry .update   an `AnsiStyle` with another's changes applied over it
  entry .StyleWord   a character's style packed into a `Long`; see `escapade.StyleWord`
    detail
        The opaque type, its bit-mask constants, `Default`, the `classTag` given, `apply`,
        `combine` and `emitDiff`, and the extension methods which read and edit a word.
    entry .FgMask   the low 24 bits: the foreground's RGB
    entry .BgMask   bits 24 to 47: the background's RGB
    entry .FgSet   bit 48: a foreground is set
    entry .BgSet   bit 49: a background is set
    entry .Bold   bit 50
    entry .Faint   bit 51
    entry .Italic   bit 52
    entry .Underline   bit 53
    entry .DoubleUnderline   bit 54
    entry .BlinkSlow   bit 55
    entry .BlinkFast   bit 56
    entry .Reverse   bit 57
    entry .Conceal   bit 58
    entry .Strike   bit 59
    entry .Overline   bit 60
    entry .HyperlinkChange   bit 61: a hyperlink opens or closes at this character
    entry .FlagsMask   every flag bit, from `FgSet` to `HyperlinkChange`
    entry .Default   `0L`: no colours, no attributes
    entry .classTag   the `ClassTag` which lets an `Array[StyleWord]` be allocated unboxed, as a `long[]`
    entry .apply   `StyleWord(raw)`: a `Long` as a style word
    entry .combine   an inner style merged under an outer one: the inner colour where set, else the outer; flags of both
    entry .emitDiff   appends to a buffer the one SGR sequence which turns a style into another, at a colour depth
        Emits `39`/`49` for a colour that was cleared, `38;2;r;g;b` or `38;5;n` (and `48…`)
        for one that changed, and the on/off code of each flag that flipped, joined with `;`
        in one `\e[…m`. Nothing is written when the two are equal.
    entry .raw   the word as a `Long`
    entry .fgRgb   the foreground's 24-bit RGB
    entry .bgRgb   the background's 24-bit RGB
    entry .hasFg   whether a foreground is set
    entry .hasBg   whether a background is set
    entry .isBold   whether the bold flag is set
    entry .isFaint   whether the faint flag is set
    entry .isItalic   whether the italic flag is set
    entry .isUnderline   whether the underline flag is set
    entry .isDoubleUnderline   whether the double-underline flag is set
    entry .isBlinkSlow   whether the slow-blink flag is set
    entry .isBlinkFast   whether the fast-blink flag is set
    entry .isReverse   whether the reverse flag is set
    entry .isConceal   whether the conceal flag is set
    entry .isStrike   whether the strike flag is set
    entry .isOverline   whether the overline flag is set
    entry .hasHyperlinkChange   whether a hyperlink opens or closes here
    entry .withFg   the word with a foreground set to an RGB value
    entry .withBg   the word with a background set to an RGB value
    entry .clearFg   the word with no foreground
    entry .clearBg   the word with no background
    entry .withBit   the word with a flag bit set
    entry .withoutBit   the word with a flag bit cleared
    entry .applyTransform   the word with the bits under a mask replaced

entry .Teletype2   an earlier styled-text representation: plain text and an `AnsiStyle` per character
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: a `Teletype` is the representation the library renders and the
      interpolator builds. `Teletype2(plain, ansi)` pairs text with an array of
      `internal.AnsiStyle` change-sets, adds with `+` (`.addable`), and renders through an
      `Ansi.Escapes` in scope. It is a top-level member of the `escapade` package but is not
      exported to the umbrella.
  entry .addable   `left + right`: the texts and style arrays concatenated
  entry #plain   the text
  entry #ansi   the style change-set of each character
  entry #render   the text with escape sequences, spelled by the `Ansi.Escapes` in scope
